# WinkUI registry parity — what you see is what installs

Consumers install **copied source** via `npx winkui add <name>` from `public/r/` (built from `registry/`). That payload must match the UI shown in docs previews (`@winkui/ui` / demos). **Never ship an older or divergent version.**

## Sources of truth (keep in sync)

| Surface | Path | Role |
|---|---|---|
| Docs preview | `packages/ui/src/components/…` | What you see on the site |
| Install payload | `registry/ui/<name>/…` (also `registry/blocks/`, `registry/charts/`) | What `registry:build` inlines into `public/r/` |
| Examples / demos | `registry/examples/<name>/…` | Must match the same latest API and look |

Allowed drift: **import paths only** (e.g. `@winkui/ui/lib/utils` vs `@/lib/utils`). Markup, variants, tokens, sizes, behavior, and a11y must be identical.

**Every variant** shown in the reference demo (default, destructive, …) must look the same in docs preview and in what `add` installs. Do not ship a “close enough” restyle.

## Required workflow after any UI edit

1. Change the component (or example) to the desired final look.
2. Mirror the same change in **both** `packages/ui` and `registry/` (and examples if they embed the UI).
3. Run `npm run registry:build` so `public/r/<item>.json` and `public/r/registry.json` pick up the new content.
4. Do **not** leave `public/r/` stale — unbuilt registry = users get the old version.

```bash
# After editing button (or any registry item):
# 1) packages/ui/src/components/button.tsx
# 2) registry/ui/button/button.tsx   # same UI, consumer imports
npm run registry:build
```

## Do / Don’t

```tsx
// ✅ Same classes, variants, tokens in both mirrors; then rebuild
// packages/ui/…/button.tsx  ↔  registry/ui/button/button.tsx

// ❌ Edit only docs package and skip registry/
// ❌ Edit registry/ but skip packages/ui (docs shows stale UI)
// ❌ Commit UI changes without npm run registry:build
// ❌ Hand-edit public/r/*.json — always regenerate from registry/
```

## Checklist (before merge / ship)

- [ ] Docs preview and `registry/` source match (except import aliases)
- [ ] Examples use the same latest API as the component
- [ ] `npm run registry:build` run after the change
- [ ] Spot-check: content in `public/r/<name>.json` matches what you saw in the browser

Human docs: `registry/README.md` · build script: `scripts/build-registry.mjs`
